WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.790
We've come a long way from Superfriends, haven't
we?

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.260
What once was a joke of a movie from Entourage
has now become a multimillion dollar, worldwide

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:08.760
hit.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.820
“The suit will not look like that on film.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.240
Tom Cruise is like 5’4 and on screen he
looks like Yao Ming.”

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.040
Yes, Aquaman is here, and it’s filled with
plenty of small nods to the wider world beyond

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.240
just DC Comics.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.360
Here are some of the small details in Aquaman
you may have missed.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.560
And if you haven't seen the movie yet, stick
some seaweed over your eyes, you dummy.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.220
There are spoilers in these waters!

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.520
When Atlanna and Tom - Aquaman's Aquamom and
Aquadad - meet for the first time, we got

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.320
a glimpse into some of the ways Tom liked
to spend his free time.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.890
You know, when he's not rescuing washed up
merwomen from the rocks down near the edge

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:43.840
of his lighthouse.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.440
If any moviegoers watched a lot of serial
children's TV in the 1960s, they might've

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.700
noticed that Tom had an episode of Stingray
playing on his television.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.880
That is, right before Atlanna threw her trident
through it once she regained consciousness.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.940
Stingray, for those who aren't familiar, concerned
a heroic submarine crew that discovers civilizations

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.100
secretly living on the ocean's floor, and
the high-adventure hijinks that ensue as a

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.260
result of that discovery.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.860
All things considered, it's a pretty fitting
television choice for Tom, if maybe a little

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:11.869
on the nose.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:16.700
Eagle eyed fans might've spotted a book featuring
the works of H.P.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.080
Lovecraft on one of Tom's tables.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.200
While Lovecraft is known for lots of weird
contributions to horror and pop culture, his

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:26.320
best known invention is probably Cthulhu,
an ancient type of cosmic sea-monster who

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.179
has a whole bunch of tentacles on his octopus-face.

00:01:29.179 --> 00:01:33.130
The various civilizations we see throughout
Aquaman - as well as the secret ocean in the

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.380
center of the world and particularly the sea-monster
who guards Atlan's trident - all seem to be

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.630
at least partially inspired by Cthulhu and
the rest of Lovecraft's creepy writing.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.680
While Aquaman director James Wan may not necessarily
be a household name just yet, fans of horror

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.060
movies are extremely familiar with his work.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.940
Wan became well known for his work on the
Conjuring movies, which feature a pair of

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.460
paranormal investigators who, well, go and
investigate the paranormal.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.640
And one of those creepy supernatural things
is the Annabelle doll, which viewers could

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:05.450
spot sitting near Mera's ship shortly after
she brings Arthur down to the ocean to begin

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:06.160
their adventure.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.760
And that's not the only reference back to
the Conjuring series of movies.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.420
Patrick Wilson, who played King Orm, has appeared
in both Conjuring films as Ed Warren, who's

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.319
based on a real-life ghost-chaser from which
the movie franchise takes its inspiration.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.340
And, while we're on the subject of Patrick
Wilson, this isn't even the first time he's

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.590
appeared in a Warner Bros. superhero movie.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.760
Back in 2009, he appeared as Nite Owl in Zack
Snyder's Watchmen adaptation.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.060
Randall Park made a couple of memorable appearances
on Aquaman's fictional TV network GBS as Doctor

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:35.880
Stephen Shin.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.520
Even for Aquaman, Shin is pretty obscure compared
with the rest of the character's comics history.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.540
Shin debuted in 2011's DC Comics reboot as
an ally of Arthurs.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.940
But Shin comes into conflict with Arthur when
the hero refuses to reveal the secret location

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:49.600
of Atlantis.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.580
Based on the film's single post-credits scene
- where Shin and Black Manta seem to team

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:57.900
up - chances seem good that Shin's role will
be expanded by quite a bit in the eventual

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.230
sequel.

